  "question": "What rule sets the Target's rest offset and row parity across landscape viewports, without a per-viewport magic number?",
  "law": {
    "statement": "restOffsetX = (compositionScale < 0.674) ? cellW/2 : 0",
    "derivation": "The canonical horizontal phase -- scrollX mod cellW, folded once when scrollY crosses a row, so the two representations of one composition collapse -- is bimodal at 0 or half a cell. It does NOT separate on orientation: the landscape 844x390 sits at half a cell and the portrait 700x900 sits at zero. It separates cleanly on composition scale. The switch is the midpoint of the gap between the two best-fitted samples either side, 500x900 at 0.654 and 1000x700 at 0.694.",
    "switch": 0.674,
    "replaces": "the v1 orientation split, which the sweep does not support",
    "verticalPhase": "one constant restY0: canonical y phase is 196-226 across every viewport measured, against cellH/2 = 210.2"
  },
